<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Creek War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a>–<a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Red Stick War · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> War of 1813 to 1814 began as a civil war among the Muscogee between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sticks">Red Sticks</a>, who resisted American expansion, and those friendly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. American militia and regulars under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> and Lower Muscogee allies, crushed the Red Sticks at Horseshoe Bend in March 1814. The Treaty of Fort Jackson forced the Muscogee to cede some 23 million acres in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/creek-war.jpg" alt="Creek War" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Reuben Chapin, 1823-1894, artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jackson_and_Weatherford.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Upper Creeks held the Coosa, Tallapoosa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> rivers, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-creek">Lower Creeks</a>, trading partners of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, had adopted more American practices. A sharp fall in the price of deerskin from 1783 to 1793 deepened debt, and treaties from 1790 to 1805 ceded Creek land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and opened the Federal Road through Creek territory. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sticks">Red Sticks</a> especially resisted the civilisation programme of the Indian agent Benjamin Hawkins. In 1811 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a> spoke for an hour to 5,000 Creeks at Tukabatchee, and the Great Comet of 1811 and the New Madrid earthquakes were taken as signs of his power. Prophets such as Josiah Francis and Peter McQueen led the war party, named for the red clubs that symbolised war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creeks</a> who opposed the war were killed, and on 22 July 1813 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sticks">Red Sticks</a> attacked the council town of Tuckabatchee. On 27 July militia intercepted a party returning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pensacola">Pensacola</a> and were defeated at the Battle of Burnt Corn. On 30 August Peter McQueen and William Weatherford attacked Fort Mims, north of Mobile, and killed 400 to 500 people, among them women and children. Spanish officials and British traders supplied the Red Sticks, who had at most 4,000 warriors and perhaps 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, Mississippi Territory and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a> all sent forces. Brigadier General John Coffee destroyed a Red Stick band at the Battle of Tallushatchee on 3 November, where women and children were among the dead, and Jackson won at Talladega on 9 November. Major General John Alexander Cocke's attack on a friendly village on 17 November became the Hillabee Massacre. Short enlistments and lack of supplies nearly dissolved Jackson's army, until the 39th United States Infantry arrived on 6 February 1814 and his force grew to about 5,000. Some 600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-creek">Lower Creek</a> allies fought with him at Horseshoe Bend on 27 March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>About 50 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> settlements were destroyed and a dozen more abandoned. Jackson made no distinction between his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-creek">Lower Creek</a> allies and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sticks">Red Sticks</a>, and the Treaty of Fort Jackson, forced on headmen of both on 9 August 1814 over the protests of the chiefs who had fought for him, took land from both, including hunting grounds claimed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a>. Jackson went on to drive the British from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pensacola">Pensacola</a> and defeat them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> on 8 January 1815. In 1818 he invaded Spanish Florida, where some Red Stick leaders had fled, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-seminole-war">First Seminole War</a>. As president he championed the Indian Removal Act of 1830, which led to the forced removal of the south-eastern peoples known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trail-of-tears">Trail of Tears</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Creek War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>Creek War</b> some 500 Cherokees fought beside Andrew Jackson against the Red Stick Creeks at the Battle of Horseshoe Bend in March 1814, John Ross among them, a service that brought them no protection when Jackson later pressed for removal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cherokee-nation">Cherokee Nation · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Creek War</b>, a large force of the Red Sticks faction under Peter McQueen and William Weatherford stormed Fort Mims, north of Mobile, Alabama, and defeated its militia garrison.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-30">30 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1783 they dealt with the United States, and Pushmataha led warriors against the Red Sticks in the <b>Creek War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/choctaw-nation">Choctaw Nation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Creek War</b> of 1813 to 1814 ended with defeat at Horseshoe Bend and huge land cessions.</blockquote><a href="/state/muscogee-confederacy">Muscogee Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
